## The short version

Build your repo's complete **front door**, merged via your own PR workflow: a README that onboards, LICENSE (chosen and justified), CONTRIBUTING (grown from your git standards), CODE_OF_CONDUCT, issue and PR templates with starter labels, a setup/onboarding guide that **survives a cold test** by someone who didn't write it, and your communication/maintainer norms. Plus 2–3 well-formed seed issues filed against your own templates.

---

## Why this assignment exists

Every stranger who ever encounters your project — a potential contributor, a curious user, a capstone faculty member, a recruiter — meets it through the same few files, in the same order, in about ninety seconds: *What is this? Can I run it? May I use it? How do I help? What's it like here?* The infrastructure package is you **designing that encounter** instead of leaving it to chance. It's the governance you chose in Weeks 2 and 8, the workflow you set in Week 7, and the welcome you intend — all made concrete enough that a stranger can act on it without ever talking to you.

The transferable lesson is one of the biggest in the course: **writing things down is how you scale yourself.** Documented processes answer questions before they're asked, make expectations enforceable without being personal, and let a project outgrow its founder's availability — which is the only way anything lasts. That's true of open-source repos, of teams inside companies, and of most organizations you'll ever help run. And the honest test of documentation isn't the author's satisfaction — it's whether a cold reader succeeds. That's why the cold test is required, and why the same test returns in Week 11 with a stranger and a grade.

---

## What to produce (all merged to main by end of Wednesday)

These eight items come from the course's required repository artifacts. The same list is also a printable checklist, with a punch-list area for tracking what's left: <file path="../../resources/CP7-Checklist.pdf" canvas_folder="Course Handouts" />

1. **README.md** — one-line what + why → status → quickstart that actually works → how to contribute (link) → license line.
2. **LICENSE** — exact text, correct fields, with the project's one-paragraph rationale recorded (in CONTRIBUTING or a docs note).
3. **CONTRIBUTING.md** — grown from your git-standards doc: how to set up, how to pick work, the PR process, review expectations, and what a newcomer should do first.
4. **CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md** — adopt a standard ([Contributor Covenant](https://www.contributor-covenant.org/version/3/0/code_of_conduct/) is the usual choice), with the reporting contact and enforcement filled in for your project.
5. **Issue templates + PR template + starter labels** (`.github/…`) — the bug-report anatomy (steps, expected vs. actual, environment) and feature-request shape built in; labels including `bug`, `enhancement`, `good first issue`.
6. **Setup/onboarding guide** — **cold-tested:** someone who didn't write it — a teammate, or on a solo project, your review partner — follows it exactly (fresh clone) and files an issue for every failure or confusion; you fix the guide and close the issues. *The closed issues stay visible — they're your evidence.*
7. **Communication & maintainer norms** (`docs/communication-norms.md`) — where discussion happens, realistic response expectations, who maintains.
8. **2–3 seed issues** filed on your own tracker, following your own templates — real next-work items.

