## Requirements & constraints

- **Everything ships via PR, cross-reviewed** — the process documents itself.
- **Adapt, don't invent** where standards exist (CoC, license text); **write, don't copy** where your project is the content (README, setup, norms).
- **Promises must be keepable.** "Reviews within a week" from a student project is credible; "24/7 support" is not. Credibility is graded.
- **The cold test is required**, not decorative — CP7's heaviest criterion is whether a stranger succeeds from docs alone.

---

## Grading

Scored on a 100-point analytic rubric; **12% of the course grade.** One grade per project; on team projects, adjusted by individual contribution.

| Criterion | Weight | What it measures |
|---|---|---|
| **Completeness** | 25 | All required artifacts present and substantive — the required-artifacts checklist above, honored. |
| **Newcomer usability** | 25 | A stranger can install, run, and start contributing from the docs alone. Verified again, for a grade, in the Week-11 audit. |
| **License appropriateness** | 15 | A *justified* choice matched to the project's goals — not MIT by reflex. |
| **Governance & comms norms** | 15 | Clear maintainer expectations, channels, and decision norms a contributor can rely on. |
| **Professionalism & consistency** | 10 | Polished and consistent — credible as a real OSS project. |
| **Contributor-readiness** | 10 | Genuinely built for others to join and build on — the course's defining thread. |

**Performance levels:** Exemplary (4) · Proficient (3) · Developing (2) · Beginning (1) · Absent (0). Score per criterion = (level ÷ 4) × weight.

